WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.834
Production firmware security depends on
more than application logic.

2
00:00:03.914 --> 00:00:07.914
Debug and maintenance interfaces should
match product risk and operational policy.

3
00:00:07.994 --> 00:00:11.884
Serviceability matters, but broad access
that is useful during development may be

4
00:00:11.964 --> 00:00:13.742
inappropriate for shipped firmware.

5
00:00:13.822 --> 00:00:17.211
Diagnostics should support supportability
without exposing secrets,

6
00:00:17.291 --> 00:00:19.958
sensitive configuration, internal trust
assumptions,

7
00:00:20.038 --> 00:00:21.983
or unnecessary implementation details.

8
00:00:22.063 --> 00:00:23.730
Logs, crash data, version reports,

9
00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:27.811
and field diagnostics should be useful
enough for operations while still respecting

10
00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:29.391
data exposure and product risk.

11
00:00:29.471 --> 00:00:32.749
Dependency review matters because crates,
features, build scripts,

12
00:00:32.829 --> 00:00:36.219
generated code, hardware abstraction
layers, board support packages,

13
00:00:36.299 --> 00:00:40.133
toolchains, linker configuration, and
platform files become part of the trusted

14
00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:40.713
firmware.

15
00:00:40.793 --> 00:00:44.349
A small feature change can pull in new
code paths or change runtime behavior.

16
00:00:44.429 --> 00:00:48.318
Release hardening should include
configuration review, artifact traceability,

17
00:00:48.398 --> 00:00:51.010
symbol and log decisions, signing-material
handling,

18
00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.701
version reporting, vulnerability response
planning,

19
00:00:53.781 --> 00:00:56.393
and evidence that production settings are
repeatable.

20
00:00:56.473 --> 00:00:58.473
Tribal knowledge is not a release process.

21
00:00:58.553 --> 00:01:00.720
Long-term maintenance also needs
ownership.

22
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.189
Teams should know who reviews dependency
updates,

23
00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:06.881
who responds to vulnerabilities, who
approves production configurations,

24
00:01:06.961 --> 00:01:10.128
who maintains release evidence, and how
field issues are reported.

25
00:01:10.208 --> 00:01:13.264
Secure firmware remains secure through
disciplined operation,

26
00:01:13.344 --> 00:01:14.900
not only through the first build.
